exam: A company of {n} children is to be conveyed across a river by means of a single boat, the capacity of which is limited to {c} children, and which cannot return without at least one occupant to row it. Determine the total number of crossings, outward and return, necessary for the conveyance of the entire company.
exam: It is given that {n} campers possess a single boat of maximum capacity {c} persons, which must at all times be rowed by one of their number. Ascertain the minimum number of crossings, counting both directions, required in order that all campers reach the opposite bank.
exam: Whereas {n} travellers are to cross a river in one boat accommodating no more than {c} persons, and whereas the boat requires a rower for every crossing including each return, state the least aggregate number of crossings.
exam: Compute the minimum number of passages of a boat across a river, whether outward or return, by which a party of {n} persons may be transferred to the far bank, the boat carrying at most {c} persons and requiring at least one person aboard on each passage.
exercise: {n+1} campers signed up, but one was 'under the weather' and stayed in the tent. The rest must cross a river in one boat that holds at most {c} people, and someone has to row it back each time. How many crossings, there and back together, does it take?
exercise: A scout leader brings {n} scouts to the river. She is 'no spring chicken' and stays on this bank with the bus. The boat holds at most {c} people, and someone must row it back. How many crossings, there and back, get all the scouts across?
test: {n+2} kids on the trip, 2 got sent home with a tummy bug (gross). boat takes {c} max n somebody has to row it back. how many crossings there n back for the rest, come on
